window.SEARCH_INDEX={"pages": [{"p": "week-01.html", "s": 0, "t": "Why does everyone need to understand data now?", "w": "Week 1 · The World of Data", "x": "Syllabus topics: Introduction, Understanding the Digital Economy, Data Skills Gap · CLO-1 LECTURE · 75 MINUTES"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 1, "t": "Activity · Is this data?", "w": "Week 1 · The World of Data", "x": "Decide for each item whether it is data, then reveal the suggested answers. SORT THE ITEMS"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 2, "t": "Introduction · What is data?", "w": "Week 1 · The World of Data", "x": "Data are recorded observations. Organised and placed in context, they become information, then knowledge that guides decisions (Rowley, 2007). Structured data fit rows and columns; unstructured data, such as text or images, do not. FROM THE LITERATURE FROM THE LITERATURE Rowley reviewed how textbooks define the data–information–knowledge–wisdom hierarchy and found broad agreement that each level is built from the one below it. FROM DATA TO DECISION"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 3, "t": "Understanding the digital economy", "w": "Week 1 · The World of Data", "x": "Bukht and Heeks (2017) define three nested scopes: the core digital sector, the narrow digital economy of digital services and platforms, and the broad digitalised economy, where any activity depends on data. THREE SCOPES OF THE DIGITAL ECONOMY · BUKHT & HEEKS (2017) HOW DATA CREATES VALUE"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 4, "t": "Activity · My data day", "w": "Week 1 · The World of Data", "x": "Open each moment of the day and note what is recorded, its type, and who holds it. Then complete the worksheet for your own day. A STUDENT'S DAY OF DATA · ILLUSTRATIVE"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 5, "t": "The data skills gap", "w": "Week 1 · The World of Data", "x": "A skills gap is the difference between the skills employers need and those workers have. Employers rank analytical thinking as the top core skill, and AI and big data as the fastest-growing (WEF, 2025). WHAT EMPLOYERS SAY · WEF FUTURE OF JOBS REPORT 2025"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 6, "t": "Exam takeaways · Week 1", "w": "Week 1 · The World of Data", "x": "Revise the mind map, then answer the likely exam questions before opening the hints. THE WEEK AS A MIND MAP · THEN LIKELY EXAM QUESTIONS What is data? Two kinds Data to decision Digital economy Skills gap Course tools Recorded facts or observations Numbers, text, images, sound Q1 Define data and give one example of structured and one of unstructured data. Q2 Explain the chain from data to decision with an everyday example. Q3 What is the digital economy? How does a free app create value from data? Q4 What is the data skills gap, and why does it matter for students from every major?"}, {"p": "week-01.html", "s": 7, "t": "Exit check", "w": "Week 1 · The World of Data", "x": "This week's lab: Lab 1 · Introduction to Course Tools and Platforms → Six questions. Feedback appears after each answer. Before Week 2: read Be Data Literate, Chapter 3. Make sure your GED106 folder is in OneDrive and shared with your partner. SIX QUESTIONS 1. Which of these is unstructured data? 2. “The noon temperature rose three days in a row” is an example of… 3. In the digital economy, data is best described as… 4. The data skills gap means… 5. In the WEF Future of Jobs Report 2025, the top core skill for employers was… 6. Which tool in this course is mainly for collecting survey responses?"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 0, "t": "What exactly does a data-literate person do?", "w": "Week 2 · The Four Pillars of Data Literacy", "x": "Syllabus topics: Explaining the 4 Characteristic of Data literacy, reading data, working with data, analysing data , Communicating with data · CLO-1 LECTURE · 77 MINUTES"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 1, "t": "Activity · Who is doing what?", "w": "Week 2 · The Four Pillars of Data Literacy", "x": "Four students use the same grades spreadsheet in four different ways. Identify what Sara is doing. VOTE"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 2, "t": "The four pillars of data literacy", "w": "Week 2 · The Four Pillars of Data Literacy", "x": "Morrow (2024) defines data literacy through four abilities: reading, working with, analysing and communicating with data. Each builds on the one before. THE FOUR PILLARS IN ORDER"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 3, "t": "Activity · One dataset, four pillars", "w": "Week 2 · The Four Pillars of Data Literacy", "x": "Apply each pillar to one week of library visits. The data are fictional. CAMPUS LIBRARY VISITS · ONE WEEK · FICTIONAL Floor Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri Floor 1 64 31 58 60 72 Floor 2 40 22 44 41 55 Floor 3 35 19 38 36 30"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 4, "t": "Communicating with data", "w": "Week 2 · The Four Pillars of Data Literacy", "x": "Readers judge position on a common scale most accurately, then length, angle and area (Cleveland & McGill, 1984). Bar charts therefore compare values better than pie charts. ACCURACY OF VISUAL JUDGEMENTS · CLEVELAND & MCGILL (1984)"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 5, "t": "Activity · Which pillar is it?", "w": "Week 2 · The Four Pillars of Data Literacy", "x": "Sort each task into its main pillar, then compare with the suggested answers. SORT THE TASKS"}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 6, "t": "Exam takeaways · Week 2", "w": "Week 2 · The Four Pillars of Data Literacy", "x": "Revise the mind map, then answer the likely exam questions before opening the hints. THE WEEK AS A MIND MAP · THEN LIKELY EXAM QUESTIONS Reading Working with Analysing Communicating Excel structure Understand tables and charts Check labels and units Q1 Name and define the four pillars of data literacy. Q2 Using one situation (e.g. library visits), give an example of each pillar. Q3 Why is communicating with data as important as analysing it? Q4 List three rules of a tidy Excel table and explain why each matters."}, {"p": "week-02.html", "s": 7, "t": "Exit check", "w": "Week 2 · The Four Pillars of Data Literacy", "x": "This week's lab: Lab 2 · Fundamentals of Microsoft Forms and Excel → Six questions. Before Week 3: read Questback, A beginner's guide to successful online surveys. Think of a topic you would like to survey your classmates about. SIX QUESTIONS 1. Cleaning duplicate rows belongs to which pillar? 2. “Why did sales fall in March?” is mainly… 3. Which is the strongest way to communicate a finding? 4. In a tidy Excel table, one row is… 5. Why is “50 AED” in a Fee column a problem? 6. Microsoft Forms is mainly used to…"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 0, "t": "How do you collect data you can trust?", "w": "Week 3 · Data Collection & Surveys", "x": "Syllabus topics: Survey design, Choosing the right Questions for a A Survey; Data Cleaning Concept · CLO-3 LECTURE · 85 MINUTES"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 1, "t": "Activity · The poll of 2.4 million people", "w": "Week 3 · Data Collection & Surveys", "x": "In 1936 the Literary Digest mailed about 10 million ballots and received about 2.4 million replies. Predict how accurate its forecast was. PREDICT"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 2, "t": "Survey design", "w": "Week 3 · Data Collection & Surveys", "x": "A survey studies a sample to describe a population. Quality depends on a clear objective, a frame that covers the population, neutral questions and a pilot test (Questback, n.d.). FIVE STEPS OF SURVEY DESIGN"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 3, "t": "Activity · Sampling bias simulator", "w": "Week 3 · Data Collection & Surveys", "x": "The 1936 Literary Digest poll received 2.4 million replies yet predicted the wrong winner. Non-response bias explained most of the error (Squire, 1988; Lusinchi, 2012). Size does not correct bias. SOURCES OF THE 19.6-POINT ERROR SELF-SELECTED VS RANDOM REPLIES · CONSTRUCTED MODEL"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 4, "t": "Choosing the right questions for a survey", "w": "Week 3 · Data Collection & Surveys", "x": "Wording changes answers. Participants who heard “smashed” estimated higher speeds than those who heard “contacted”, for the same film (Loftus & Palmer, 1974). SAME FILM, DIFFERENT VERB · LOFTUS & PALMER (1974) DIAGNOSE EACH QUESTION"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 5, "t": "The data cleaning concept", "w": "Week 3 · Data Collection & Surveys", "x": "Cleaning fixes inconsistent labels, formats, missing values, duplicates and impossible values before analysis. The goal is tidy data: one variable per column, one observation per row (Wickham, 2014). FIVE RESPONSES · BEFORE AND AFTER # City Age Major 1 Dubai 19 Business 2 dubai␣ nineteen business 3 DXB 20 4 Sharjah 230 Design 5 Dubai 19 Business"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 6, "t": "Exam takeaways · Week 3", "w": "Week 3 · Data Collection & Surveys", "x": "Revise the mind map, then answer the likely exam questions before opening the hints. THE WEEK AS A MIND MAP · THEN LIKELY EXAM QUESTIONS Design steps Sampling bias Question faults Question types Cleaning concept Objective Population and sample Questions → pilot → collect Q1 Explain the difference between a population and a sample, with an example. Q2 What went wrong with the 1936 Literary Digest poll? Q3 Rewrite this question: “How often do you skip breakfast before class?” Q4 What is data cleaning, and why is it an ethical as well as a technical task?"}, {"p": "week-03.html", "s": 7, "t": "Exit check", "w": "Week 3 · Data Collection & Surveys", "x": "This week's lab: Lab 3 · Survey Design Using Microsoft Forms → Six questions. Before Week 4: read Be Data Literate, Chapter 2. Make sure your group's survey is ready to send. SIX QUESTIONS 1. A survey sent only to students in the library at 9 pm has… 2. Increasing a biased sample from 10,000 to 1,000,000… 3. “How satisfied are you with the food and the prices?” is… 4. Which question type is easiest to count and chart? 5. “dubai ”, “Dubai” and “DXB” in one column is a problem of… 6. The first step of survey design is…"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 0, "t": "From “what happened?” to “what should we do?”", "w": "Week 4 · The Four Levels of Analytics", "x": "Syllabus topics: Descriptive analytics; Diagnostic analytics; Predictive analytics; Prescriptive analytics · CLO-1 LECTURE · 80 MINUTES"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 1, "t": "Activity · What is the manager really asking?", "w": "Week 4 · The Four Levels of Analytics", "x": "A gym manager asks a question about next month. Identify which kind of question it is. VOTE"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 2, "t": "The four levels of analytics", "w": "Week 4 · The Four Levels of Analytics", "x": "Descriptive analytics reports what happened, diagnostic explains why, predictive estimates what will happen, and prescriptive recommends an action. Prescriptive is the least mature level (Lepenioti et al., 2020). THE ANALYTICS LADDER"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 3, "t": "Activity · One building, four levels", "w": "Week 4 · The Four Levels of Analytics", "x": "Open each level in order on 14 days of fictional campus energy data. In Predictive, try a temperature outside the data range. CAMPUS BUILDING ENERGY · 14 DAYS · FICTIONAL"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 4, "t": "Activity · Which level is it?", "w": "Week 4 · The Four Levels of Analytics", "x": "Sort each question into the level of analytics it requires. SORT THE QUESTIONS"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 5, "t": "When analytics goes wrong", "w": "Week 4 · The Four Levels of Analytics", "x": "Google Flu Trends predicted flu from search queries. In 2012–13 it estimated more than double the official rate, because search behaviour had changed (Lazer et al., 2014). FROM THE LITERATURE FROM THE LITERATURE Google Flu Trends’ peak estimate for 2012–13 was more than double the CDC figure. WHY PREDICTIONS FAIL"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 6, "t": "Exam takeaways · Week 4", "w": "Week 4 · The Four Levels of Analytics", "x": "Revise the mind map, then answer the likely exam questions before opening the hints. THE WEEK AS A MIND MAP · THEN LIKELY EXAM QUESTIONS Descriptive Diagnostic Predictive Prescriptive Lessons What happened? Totals, averages, charts Q1 Name the four levels of analytics and the question each answers. Q2 Give one example of each level for a single organisation (e.g. a university library). Q3 Why is prescriptive analytics the most valuable and the most risky? Q4 What can we learn from Google Flu Trends?"}, {"p": "week-04.html", "s": 7, "t": "Exit check", "w": "Week 4 · The Four Levels of Analytics", "x": "This week's lab: Lab 4 · Data Collection and Exporting → Six questions. Before Week 5: read Be Data Literate, Chapter 5. The syllabus schedules an in-class quiz in Week 5: revise Weeks 1–4 using the Exam takeaways scenes. SIX QUESTIONS 1. “The building used 57 MWh a day on average last month” is… 2. “Energy use was higher on hotter days” mainly helps answer… 3. Which level recommends an action? 4. Predictions are least reliable when… 5. Google Flu Trends shows that… 6. Before analysing a Forms export, you should first…"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 0, "t": "Can you read a chart as carefully as a sentence?", "w": "Week 5 · Reading and Speaking the Language of Data", "x": "Syllabus topics: Defining Reading data, Data fluency, The data dictionary · CLO-1 LECTURE · 85 MINUTES"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 1, "t": "Activity · Is Cafeteria B really 2.5 times better?", "w": "Week 5 · Reading and Speaking the Language of Data", "x": "Compare the two cafeterias, then switch the axis. SAME DATA, TWO AXES · FICTIONAL"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 2, "t": "Defining reading data", "w": "Week 5 · Reading and Speaking the Language of Data", "x": "A truncated y-axis makes differences look larger than they are (Pandey et al., 2015). Check title, axes, units, scale and source before interpreting a chart. FROM THE LITERATURE FROM THE LITERATURE Participants who saw bar charts with a truncated y-axis rated the difference between bars as larger than participants who saw the same data from zero. FIVE CHECKS BEFORE YOU BELIEVE A CHART"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 3, "t": "Data fluency · speaking the language of data", "w": "Week 5 · Reading and Speaking the Language of Data", "x": "Data fluency is using data terms accurately (Morrow, 2024). Common confusions: mean and median, percent and percentage points, correlation and causation. 12 WORDS OF DATA FLUENCY"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 4, "t": "Activity · Mean or median?", "w": "Week 5 · Reading and Speaking the Language of Data", "x": "Change the ninth salary and compare the mean with the median. For skewed data, the median better describes a typical value. NINE SALARIES · ILLUSTRATIVE"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 5, "t": "The data dictionary", "w": "Week 5 · Reading and Speaking the Language of Data", "x": "A data dictionary records each field's name, type, unit, allowed values, definition and source (Morrow, 2024). DATA DICTIONARY · LAB 4 SURVEY FILE Field name Major Data type Text (categorical) Unit — Allowed values Business, Design, Engineering, Computing, Media, Other Description Student's declared major, chosen from a list in the survey Source Survey question Q1"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 6, "t": "In-class quiz", "w": "Week 5 · Reading and Speaking the Language of Data", "x": "Warm-up on Weeks 1–4. For full practice, see Practice Quiz 1. WARM-UP · FOUR QUESTIONS 1. Data → information → knowledge → … 2. Which is NOT one of the four pillars? 3. A bigger biased sample… 4. “What should we do?” is which level of analytics?"}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 7, "t": "Exam takeaways · Week 5", "w": "Week 5 · Reading and Speaking the Language of Data", "x": "Revise the mind map, then answer the likely exam questions before opening the hints. THE WEEK AS A MIND MAP · THEN LIKELY EXAM QUESTIONS Reading data Data fluency Key terms Mix-ups Data dictionary What it says and does not say Title, axes, scale Source, message, limits Q1 Define reading data and data fluency. How are they different? Q2 A bar chart's axis starts at 70%. Why can this mislead? Q3 When is the median better than the mean? Give an example. Q4 What is a data dictionary? Write one entry for a field called “Hours_studied”."}, {"p": "week-05.html", "s": 8, "t": "Exit check", "w": "Week 5 · Reading and Speaking the Language of Data", "x": "This week's lab: Lab 5 · Sorting and Filtering Data → Six questions. Before Week 6: read Be Data Literate, Chapter 8. Bring your Lab 4 Excel file: next week we clean it. SIX QUESTIONS 1. Support rose from 40% to 50%. That is… 2. One billionaire joins a group of nine people. Which measure barely changes? 3. The first thing to check on a bar chart is… 4. “Major” with values Business, Design, Media is… 5. Which is NOT usually part of a data dictionary entry? 6. In Excel, a filter…"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 0, "t": "What kind of thinker does good data need?", "w": "Week 6 · The Three Cs of Data Literacy", "x": "Syllabus topics: Curiosity, Creativity, and Critical thinking · CLO-1 LECTURE · 75 MINUTES"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 1, "t": "Activity · “Sit in the front row, get better grades”", "w": "Week 6 · The Three Cs of Data Literacy", "x": "Read the claim and choose your first reaction. YOUR FIRST REACTION"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 2, "t": "Curiosity", "w": "Week 6 · The Three Cs of Data Literacy", "x": "Curiosity means questioning data. In 1854 John Snow mapped cholera deaths in London and traced them to one water pump (Snow, 1855). HOW A CURIOUS MIND WORKS"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 3, "t": "Creativity", "w": "Week 6 · The Three Cs of Data Literacy", "x": "Creativity means new ways to show data. Nightingale's 1858 diagrams showed most Crimean War deaths came from preventable disease, which supported hospital reform. SAME DATA, FOUR VIEWS · FICTIONAL MODE STUDENTS Car (driven by me) 38 Metro 22 Bus / shuttle 18 Taxi or ride-hail 14 Walk or bike 8"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 4, "t": "Critical thinking", "w": "Week 6 · The Three Cs of Data Literacy", "x": "Chocolate consumption correlates with Nobel prizes across 23 countries (Messerli, 2012). Correlation alone does not prove cause: national wealth may drive both. FROM THE LITERATURE FROM THE LITERATURE Chocolate consumption and Nobel laureates per 10 million people, 23 countries (p < 0.0001). ASK WHY Who collected it, and why? Motive can shape the data How many, and who? Small or biased samples Compared with what? A number alone means little Correlation or cause? A third factor may drive both What is missing? Hidden groups, years or variables"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 5, "t": "Activity · Claim checker", "w": "Week 6 · The Three Cs of Data Literacy", "x": "Decide whether to accept, question or reject each constructed claim. ACCEPT, QUESTION OR REJECT?"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 6, "t": "Exam takeaways · Week 6", "w": "Week 6 · The Three Cs of Data Literacy", "x": "Revise the mind map, then answer the likely exam questions before opening the hints. THE WEEK AS A MIND MAP · THEN LIKELY EXAM QUESTIONS Curiosity Creativity Critical thinking Data cleaning Good habits Ask what, why, who is missing John Snow 1854 cholera map Q1 Name the three Cs of data literacy and give an example of each. Q2 “Ice cream sales cause sunburn.” Use critical thinking to respond. Q3 List four data-cleaning problems and the Excel tool for each. Q4 Why should you flag a missing value instead of filling it in?"}, {"p": "week-06.html", "s": 7, "t": "Exit check", "w": "Week 6 · The Three Cs of Data Literacy", "x": "This week's lab: Lab 6 · Data Cleaning and Preparation → Six questions. Before Week 7: read Be Data Literate, Chapter 4, and Cote (2021), 5 principles of data ethics for business professionals (Harvard Business School Online). SIX QUESTIONS 1. Asking “who is missing from this data?” shows… 2. John Snow's 1854 map is a classic example of… 3. Two things rise together because a third factor drives both. This means… 4. Which Excel tool removes extra spaces at the start and end of text? 5. A respondent left “Hours” blank. Best practice is to… 6. Before cleaning, you should…"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 0, "t": "Just because we can collect it, should we?", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "Syllabus topics: Principles of data Ethics: Ownership; Transparency; Privacy; Intention; Outcomes · CLO-1 LECTURE · 84 MINUTES"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 1, "t": "Activity · A free coffee for your location?", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "Decide whether you would share your location for a free coffee. VOTE"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 2, "t": "The five principles of data ethics", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "Cote (2021) lists five principles of data ethics: ownership, transparency, privacy, intention and outcomes. CLICK EACH PRINCIPLE"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 3, "t": "Ownership and transparency", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "Facebook Likes alone predicted traits users never disclosed (Kosinski et al., 2013). Informed consent requires that people know what is collected and how it is used. WHAT LIKES REVEALED · KOSINSKI ET AL. (2013)"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 4, "t": "Privacy · is “no names” anonymous?", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "Removing names does not anonymise data. Four location points identified 95% of 1.5 million mobile users (de Montjoye et al., 2013). HOW UNIQUE IS EACH ROW? · SYNTHETIC DATA"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 5, "t": "Intention and outcomes", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "Intention asks why data are collected; outcomes asks what happens to people as a result. A good purpose does not guarantee a fair result. ASK BEFORE, CHECK AFTER"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 6, "t": "Regulatory impact · UAE personal data protection", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021 governs personal data in the UAE. Consent is the default basis; individuals can access, correct and erase their data. ETHICS PRINCIPLE → WHAT THE UAE LAW ASKS PRINCIPLE IN THE UAE LAW Ownership Consent is the default basis Transparency People can access information about their data Privacy Security duties; rights to correct and erase Intention Process for a specific, clear purpose Outcomes Right to object to automated processing"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 7, "t": "Activity · Which principle is at stake?", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "Match each case to the principle most at stake. MATCH THE CASES"}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 8, "t": "Exam takeaways · Week 7", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "Revise the mind map, then answer the likely exam questions before opening the hints. THE WEEK AS A MIND MAP · THEN LIKELY EXAM QUESTIONS Ownership Transparency Privacy Intention Outcomes UAE law People own their data Active, clear consent Q1 List and explain the five principles of data ethics. Q2 Why is removing names not enough to make data anonymous? Q3 Use Cambridge Analytica to discuss two principles. Q4 Explain the difference between intention and outcomes with one example."}, {"p": "week-07.html", "s": 9, "t": "Exit check", "w": "Week 7 · Data Ethics -1", "x": "This week's lab: Lab 7 · Basic Analytical Functions in Excel → Six questions. Before Week 8: re-read Be Data Literate, Chapter 4. The Midterm is on Friday 23 October, 3 PM: use the midterm review page. SIX QUESTIONS 1. In de Montjoye et al. (2013), how many location points identified 95% of users? 2. Which is the best summary of “transparency”? 3. Sweeney (2000) showed that… 4. “Good intentions, unfair results” is a problem of… 5. Facebook Likes predicted political party with about what accuracy (Kosinski et al., 2013)? 6. =COUNTIF(B2:B9,\"Business\") returns…"}, {"p": "week-08.html", "s": 0, "t": "How does data harm people when nobody means to?", "w": "Week 8 · Data Ethics -2", "x": "Syllabus topic: Common Ethical Problems in Data · CLO-1 TODAY'S SESSION · 120 MINUTES"}, {"p": "week-08.html", "s": 1, "t": "Activity · Can a computer be biased?", "w": "Week 8 · Data Ethics -2", "x": "Decide whether a system that follows its data exactly can still produce unfair results. VOTE"}, {"p": "week-08.html", "s": 2, "t": "Problem 1 · Biased data", "w": "Week 8 · Data Ethics -2", "x": "Biased data under-represent some groups. Commercial gender classifiers erred up to 34.7% for darker-skinned women but at most 0.8% for lighter-skinned men (Buolamwini & Gebru, 2018). ERROR RATES · BUOLAMWINI & GEBRU (2018)"}, {"p": "week-08.html", "s": 3, "t": "Problem 2 · Proxy variables", "w": "Week 8 · Data Ethics -2", "x": "A proxy stands in for what we want to measure. An algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for need, which under-selected Black patients (Obermeyer et al., 2019). OBERMEYER ET AL. (2019) WHO GETS THE CARE PLACES? · CONSTRUCTED DATA"}, {"p": "week-08.html", "s": 4, "t": "Problem 3 · Misleading presentation", "w": "Week 8 · Data Ethics -2", "x": "True numbers can mislead through a cherry-picked time window, a truncated axis or a missing comparison. Find a window on the right that reverses the trend. PICK A WINDOW, WRITE A HEADLINE · FICTIONAL DATA"}, {"p": "week-08.html", "s": 5, "t": "Problems 4 and 5 · Privacy, security and over-collection", "w": "Week 8 · Data Ethics -2", "x": "Collect only what the purpose requires and limit who can open the data. The table summarises this week's five problems. FIVE PROBLEMS AND THEIR CHECKS PROBLEM CHECK Biased data Who is missing from the data? Proxy variable Are we measuring what we care about? Misleading presentation Full range, honest axis, a comparison Privacy and security Who can identify or open the data? Over-collection Is every field needed?"}, {"p": "week-08.html", "s": 6, "t": "Activity · Ethics check for your project", "w": "Week 8 · Data Ethics -2", "x": "Run the five checks on your group project survey. FIVE CHECKS · ONE PER PROBLEM"}, {"p": "week-08.html", "s": 7, "t": "Exam takeaways · Week 8", "w": "Week 8 · Data Ethics -2", "x": "Revise the mind map, then answer the likely exam questions before opening the hints. THE WEEK AS A MIND MAP · THEN LIKELY EXAM QUESTIONS Biased data Proxy variables Misleading Privacy, security Over-collection Repeats past unfairness Gender Shades 2018 Q1 Explain biased data with a real example. Q2 What is a proxy variable, and why can it cause unfair results? Q3 How can true numbers mislead? Give two techniques. Q4 Give three ethics checks you would run on a student survey project."}, {"p": "week-08.html", "s": 8, "t": "Exit check", "w": "Week 8 · Data Ethics -2", "x": "Four quick questions. Then work through the practice midterm for the rest of the session. After the midterm: read Be Data Literate, Chapter 9, for Week 9 (Data-Informed Decision-Making). FOUR QUESTIONS 1. An algorithm uses past spending to decide who needs care. This is a problem of… 2. Showing only the two months when sales fell, from a year of growth, is… 3. In Gender Shades (2018), classifiers made the most errors for… 4. The best response to over-collection is to…"}, {"p": "lab-01.html", "s": 0, "t": "Lab 1 · Introduction to Course Tools and Platforms", "w": "Lab 1 · Lab 1 · Introduction to Course Tools and Platforms", "x": "Set up Microsoft 365 environment. Navigate and familiarize with Microsoft Excel, Forms, and OneDrive. Follow the five steps, then complete the checklist. FIVE SET-UP STEPS · THEN THE CHECKLIST"}, {"p": "lab-01.html", "s": 1, "t": "Before you leave", "w": "Lab 1 · Lab 1 · Introduction to Course Tools and Platforms", "x": "Submit on Moodle: GED106 folder in OneDrive, shared with your partner, and the checklist. Save your files in OneDrive › GED106 › Labs."}, {"p": "lab-02.html", "s": 0, "t": "Lab 2 · Fundamentals of Microsoft Forms and Excel", "w": "Lab 2 · Lab 2 · Fundamentals of Microsoft Forms and Excel", "x": "Explore interface features and core functionalities in Microsoft Forms (survey design) and Excel (data structures). In Forms, add a Choice, Text, Rating and Date question. In Excel, fix the messy table using the six rules. MESSY VS TIDY · SIX RULES A B C D 1 CLUB MEMBERS SPRING (merged) 2 Name Major Fee Events 3 Aisha Business 50 AED Hike, Quiz night 4 5 Omar bus. 50 Quiz night 6 TOTAL 100 7 Lina Design free? Hike Rule 1 · One header row, no merged cells Rule 2 · One row = one record Rule 3 · One column = one variable Rule 4 · Numbers stay numbers Rule 5 · Same spelling every time Rule 6 · No totals inside the data"}, {"p": "lab-02.html", "s": 1, "t": "Before you leave", "w": "Lab 2 · Lab 2 · Fundamentals of Microsoft Forms and Excel", "x": "Submit on Moodle: your test form and Lab02_YourName.xlsx. Save your files in OneDrive › GED106 › Labs."}, {"p": "lab-03.html", "s": 0, "t": "Lab 3 · Survey Design Using Microsoft Forms", "w": "Lab 3 · Lab 3 · Survey Design Using Microsoft Forms", "x": "Construct a basic survey instrument targeting specific data collection objectives. Plan in the worksheet, build 6–10 questions in Forms, and pilot the survey with another group. BUILD IT IN FORMS"}, {"p": "lab-03.html", "s": 1, "t": "Before you leave", "w": "Lab 3 · Lab 3 · Survey Design Using Microsoft Forms", "x": "Submit on Moodle: the survey plan and the link to your survey. Save your files in OneDrive › GED106 › Labs."}, {"p": "lab-04.html", "s": 0, "t": "Lab 4 · Data Collection and Exporting", "w": "Lab 4 · Lab 4 · Data Collection and Exporting", "x": "Simulate and record responses. Download and format structured data for Excel-based analysis. Collect at least 15 responses, then follow the five steps. A TYPICAL FORMS EXPORT · THEN THE STEPS ID Start time Completion time Email Name Q1 Major Q2 Hours studied Q3 Rating 1 10/14 17:02 10/14 17:05 anonymous Business 6 4 2 10/14 17:03 10/14 17:04 anonymous Design 10 5"}, {"p": "lab-04.html", "s": 1, "t": "Before you leave", "w": "Lab 4 · Lab 4 · Data Collection and Exporting", "x": "Submit on Moodle: Lab04_GroupName.xlsx, with no names or emails. Save your files in OneDrive › GED106 › Labs."}, {"p": "lab-05.html", "s": 0, "t": "Lab 5 · Sorting and Filtering Data", "w": "Lab 5 · Lab 5 · Sorting and Filtering Data", "x": "Apply Excel’s sorting, filtering, and conditional formatting tools to explore and manage datasets. Practise on the table, then repeat in Excel on your Lab 4 file. PRACTISE · SORT, FILTER, HIGHLIGHT Name ↕ Major ↕ Year ↕ Hours ↕ Quiz ↕ Aisha Business 2 6 78 Omar Design 1 10 85 Lina Computing 3 4 52 Yousef Business 1 12 91 Mariam Media 2 7 66 Khalid Computing 2 3 48 Sara Design 3 9 88 Hamdan Business 4 5 59 Noor Media 1 11 81 Rami Engineering 2 8 73 Fatima Engineering 3 6 69 Zayed Design 2 2 41"}, {"p": "lab-05.html", "s": 1, "t": "Before you leave", "w": "Lab 5 · Lab 5 · Sorting and Filtering Data", "x": "Submit on Moodle: Lab05_YourName.xlsx and three sentences on what you found. Save your files in OneDrive › GED106 › Labs."}, {"p": "lab-06.html", "s": 0, "t": "Lab 6 · Data Cleaning and Preparation", "w": "Lab 6 · Lab 6 · Data Cleaning and Preparation", "x": "Identify and address inconsistencies, missing values, and outliers to ensure data quality. Clean the sample, then your own file: keep the raw sheet, clean in a Clean sheet, and record every change in a Log sheet. CLEAN THE DATA · FICTIONAL RESPONSES ID City Age Major Hours 1 Dubai 19 Business 6 2 dubai␣ 20 Design 10 3 DXB 21 bus. ten 4 Sharjah 230 Computing 4 5 Dubai 19 Business (blank) 6 SHARJAH 22 Media 7 7 Ajman 20 Design 9 8 dubai␣ 20 Design 10 9 Abu Dhabi 23 Business 60 10 Dubai 18 Computing 5"}, {"p": "lab-06.html", "s": 1, "t": "Before you leave", "w": "Lab 6 · Lab 6 · Data Cleaning and Preparation", "x": "Submit on Moodle: your file with Clean and Log sheets (graded lab assignment). Save your files in OneDrive › GED106 › Labs."}, {"p": "lab-07.html", "s": 0, "t": "Lab 7 · Basic Analytical Functions in Excel", "w": "Lab 7 · Lab 7 · Basic Analytical Functions in Excel", "x": "Use built-in Excel formulas (e.g., AVERAGE, COUNTIF, IF, VLOOKUP) for basic data computation and logical operations. Download the workbook and save a copy as Lab07_YourName.xlsx. WORKBOOK SHEET WHAT IT HOLDS Tasks Questions A1–E1. Type your formulas in the yellow cells. Data 40 students (fictional), rows 2–41. Columns H–J are for you to fill. Ratings Lookup table for Part D. Do not edit. LAB PLAN · 60 MINUTES"}, {"p": "lab-07.html", "s": 1, "t": "Part A · AVERAGE", "w": "Lab 7 · Lab 7 · Basic Analytical Functions in Excel", "x": "=AVERAGE(range) returns the mean. A1 is done for you; complete A2 (Study_Hours) and A3 (Satisfaction). TRY IT · 8 SAMPLE ROWS A Name B Major C Quiz D Hours 2 Aisha Business 78 6 3 Omar Design 85 10 4 Lina Computing 52 4 5 Yousef Business 91 12 6 Mariam Media 66 7 7 Khalid Computing 48 3 8 Sara Design 88 9 9 Hamdan Business 59 5"}, {"p": "lab-07.html", "s": 2, "t": "Part B · COUNTIF", "w": "Lab 7 · Lab 7 · Basic Analytical Functions in Excel", "x": "=COUNTIF(range, criteria) counts cells that meet one condition; put text and conditions in quotes. Complete B1–B3. TRY IT · CHANGE THE CONDITION A Name B Major C Quiz D Hours 2 Aisha Business 78 6 3 Omar Design 85 10 4 Lina Computing 52 4 5 Yousef Business 91 12 6 Mariam Media 66 7 7 Khalid Computing 48 3 8 Sara Design 88 9 9 Hamdan Business 59 5"}, {"p": "lab-07.html", "s": 3, "t": "Part C · IF", "w": "Lab 7 · Lab 7 · Basic Analytical Functions in Excel", "x": "=IF(test, if_true, if_false) returns one of two results. Fill Data!H (Pass if Quiz_Score ≥ 60) and Data!I (High if Study_Hours ≥ 10), then complete C3 and C4. TRY IT · MOVE THE PASS MARK A Name B Major C Quiz D Hours E Result 2 Aisha Business 78 6 Pass 3 Omar Design 85 10 Pass 4 Lina Computing 52 4 Fail 5 Yousef Business 91 12 Pass 6 Mariam Media 66 7 Pass 7 Khalid Computing 48 3 Fail 8 Sara Design 88 9 Pass 9 Hamdan Business 59 5 Fail"}, {"p": "lab-07.html", "s": 4, "t": "Part D · VLOOKUP", "w": "Lab 7 · Lab 7 · Basic Analytical Functions in Excel", "x": "=VLOOKUP(value, table, column, FALSE) finds a value in the first column of a table. In Data!J2, look up F2 in Ratings!$A$2:$B$6, fill down, then complete D2. TRY IT · LOOKUP TABLE ON THE RIGHT A Name B Major C Quiz D Hours E Code 2 Aisha Business 78 6 BUS 3 Omar Design 85 10 DES 4 Lina Computing 52 4 CSC 5 Yousef Business 91 12 BUS 6 Mariam Media 66 7 MED 7 Khalid Computing 48 3 CSC 8 Sara Design 88 9 DES 9 Hamdan Business 59 5 BUS"}, {"p": "lab-07.html", "s": 5, "t": "Part E · Interpret and submit", "w": "Lab 7 · Lab 7 · Basic Analytical Functions in Excel", "x": "Write two sentences in E1 using at least two numbers, then submit Lab07_YourName.xlsx on Moodle. COMMON ERRORS #DIV/0! AVERAGE found no numbers. Check the range, or numbers stored as text. #N/A VLOOKUP found no match. Check spelling and use FALSE. #NAME? Typo in the function name, or text without quotes. Wrong after fill down Lock the lookup range with $: $A$2:$B$6."}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 0, "t": "Midterm · Friday 23 October, 3:00–4:30 PM", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Hub Zones · 90 minutes · closed book · 20 marks. The midterm covers Weeks 1–8. Start with the terms students most often confuse. OFTEN CONFUSED OFTEN CONFUSED THE DIFFERENCE Data · information Raw values · values organised with context Population · sample Everyone you study · those you actually ask Sampling bias · non-response bias Who is asked · who chooses to reply Leading · loaded question Pushes an answer · assumes a fact Mean · median Sum ÷ count · middle value (robust to outliers) Percent · percentage points Relative change · difference between two percentages Correlation · causation Move together · one changes the other Predictive · prescriptive What will happen · what we should do Transparency · privacy Telling people how data are used · protecting the data Intention · outcomes Why data are collected · what happens to people Anonymised · de-identified Cannot be linked to a person · names removed, may still be linked"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 1, "t": "Weeks 1–4", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Cover a column and recall it. WEEKS 1–4 WEEK 1 WEEK 2 WEEK 3 WEEK 4 Key idea Data → information → knowledge Four pillars Fair sample, fair questions Four levels of analytics Key terms Structured, digital economy, skills gap Reading, working with, analysing, communicating Population, sample, non-response bias, tidy data Descriptive, diagnostic, predictive, prescriptive Evidence Rowley (2007); WEF (2025) Cleveland & McGill (1984) Squire (1988); Loftus & Palmer (1974) Lazer et al. (2014)"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 2, "t": "Weeks 5–8", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Cover a column and recall it. WEEKS 5–8 WEEK 5 WEEK 6 WEEK 7 WEEK 8 Key idea Read before you believe Curiosity, creativity, critical thinking Five ethics principles Common ethical problems Key terms Mean, median, percentage points, data dictionary Correlation vs causation Consent, re-identification Biased data, proxy, cherry-picking Evidence Pandey et al. (2015) Snow (1855); Messerli (2012) Sweeney (2000); Kosinski et al. (2013) Buolamwini & Gebru (2018); Obermeyer et al. (2019)"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 3, "t": "Key terms", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Switch on Self-test and work through the cards. 20 TERMS"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 4, "t": "Practice questions", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Answer on paper first, then open the hint. NINE QUESTIONS Q1 Distinguish data from information, with one example. Q2 What is a data skills gap? Q3 Why did the 1936 Literary Digest poll fail despite 2.4 million replies? Q4 What is tidy data? Give two rules. Q5 Which better describes a typical salary, the mean or the median? Why? Q6 List four things a data dictionary records. Q7 Chocolate consumption and Nobel prizes correlate (r = 0.79). What can you conclude? Q8 Why does removing names not make a dataset anonymous? Q9 What is a proxy variable? Give a published example."}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 5, "t": "Mixed quiz", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Ten questions across Weeks 1–8. TEN QUESTIONS 1. A voice note is… 2. Removing duplicate rows belongs to which pillar? 3. Readers judge which encoding most accurately? 4. The main cause of the 1936 Literary Digest error was… 5. Loftus & Palmer (1974) showed that… 6. Support rose from 40% to 50%. That is… 7. One very high value is added. Which measure moves most? 8. Google Flu Trends failed mainly because… 9. Sweeney (2000) estimated that ZIP code, gender and birth date identify about… 10. Showing only the months that support your story is…"}, {"p": "review.html", "s": 6, "t": "Excel functions", "w": "Midterm review · Midterm Review", "x": "Read each formula aloud, then check its meaning. THREE FORMULAS =COUNTIF(C2:C40,\">=70\") =IF(D2=\"\",\"Missing\",\"OK\") =VLOOKUP(A2,Majors!A:B,2,FALSE)"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 0, "t": "Midterm practice", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Five parts, 30 questions each. Answer first; the model answer explains why each option is right or wrong. All data are fictional. PARTS 1 Four levels of analytics 2 Reading data from tables and charts 3 MCQs: data dictionary, data literacy, data protection, surveys 4 Survey questions: find the error and fix it 5 Five principles of data ethics"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 1, "t": "Part 1 · Four levels of analytics (1–10)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Name the level of analytics. QUESTIONS 1–10 1. “Average emergency-room waiting time last month was 42 minutes.” 2. “Absences rose in March, mainly because of a flu outbreak on campus.” 3. “The model estimates that about 4,200 new students will enrol next year.” 4. “Send a reminder to any student who misses a second lab.” 5. “The library lent 3,150 books in September.” 6. “What explains the longer emergency-room waits on Friday nights?” 7. “This patient has a 70% risk of readmission within 30 days.” 8. “Schedule two extra nurses on Friday nights to cut waiting times.” 9. “45% of respondents use the metro to reach campus.” 10. “Wards without hand-gel stations show the highest infection rates, which points to the cause.”"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 2, "t": "Part 1 · Four levels of analytics (11–20)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Name the level of analytics. QUESTIONS 11–20 1. “The weather model forecasts 43 °C in Dubai tomorrow.” 2. “Route each delivery driver along the fastest path given current traffic.” 3. “Rainfall in Fujairah totalled 120 mm in January.” 4. “Library visits fell when opening hours were shortened; the two dates match.” 5. “Students who miss three labs by Week 6 are likely to fail the course.” 6. “Set the air conditioning to 24 °C in empty rooms to reduce energy use.” 7. “What share of students passed the statistics course in Fall?” 8. “A drill-down shows most failed log-ins came from one outdated app version.” 9. “Demand for flights to Muscat is projected to rise 15% during Eid.” 10. “The system recommends which flights to overbook, and by how many seats.”"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 3, "t": "Part 1 · Four levels of analytics (21–30)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Name the level of analytics. QUESTIONS 21–30 1. “The dashboard shows each building’s energy use in 2025.” 2. “The team investigates whether the exam timetable explains low attendance in Week 9.” 3. “Which customers are likely to cancel their phone contract next quarter?” 4. “Move the statistics exam to the morning slot, where attendance is highest.” 5. “The airport’s on-time rate was 81% in Q2.” 6. “Root-cause analysis links the power cut to an overloaded transformer.” 7. “Water use in the dorms will probably exceed capacity by 2028.” 8. “Offer the vaccine first to patients with the highest predicted risk.” 9. “Report the number of patients admitted to each ward in 2025.” 10. “Restock umbrellas in stores where rain is forecast this week.”"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 4, "t": "Part 2 · Reading data (1–10)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Use Table 1 and Chart 1. DATA ACTIVITY FALL SPRING Swimming 1,240 1,180 Football 980 1,150 Gym 2,310 2,420 Yoga 450 360 1. Table 1: total visits in Fall? 2. Table 1: change in total visits from Fall to Spring? 3. Table 1: percentage change for Yoga? 4. Table 1: which activities had fewer visits in Spring? 5. Table 1: Gym’s share of Spring visits? 6. Chart 1: which time band has the longest average wait? 7. Chart 1: difference between the longest and shortest wait? 8. Chart 1: mean of the four bands? 9. Chart 1: how many bands are above 40 minutes? 10. Chart 1 alone: can you conclude that night waits are longer because fewer staff work at night?"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 5, "t": "Part 2 · Reading data (11–20)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Use Table 2 and Chart 2. DATA 52 61 64 67 70 72 75 78 98 1. Table 2: median score? 2. Table 2: mean score (one decimal)? 3. Table 2: range? 4. Table 2: if the 98 is removed, which measure changes more? 5. Table 2: how many students scored 70 or more? 6. Chart 2: increase from 2019 to 2024? 7. Chart 2: largest one-year increase? 8. Chart 2: percentage increase from 2023 to 2024? 9. Chart 2: average yearly increase, 2019–2024? 10. Chart 2: what can you say about 2025?"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 6, "t": "Part 2 · Reading data (21–30)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Use Tables 3 and 4. DATA MODE YEAR 1 YEAR 4 Car 30 55 Metro 25 20 Bus 35 15 Walk / other 10 10 SLEEP PER NIGHT STUDENTS AVERAGE SCORE Under 6 h 30 64 6–8 h 70 73 Over 8 h 20 71 1. Table 3: most common mode for Year 1? 2. Table 3: difference in car use between Year 4 and Year 1? 3. Table 3: relative to Year 1, Year 4 car use is… 4. Table 3: Year 4 bus share compared with Year 1? 5. Table 3: can you tell how many Year 4 students drive? 6. Table 4: which group is largest? 7. Table 4: what percentage of students sleep under 6 hours? 8. Table 4: score difference between 6–8 h and under 6 h? 9. Table 4: overall average score of all 120 students? 10. Table 4: does this prove that more sleep causes higher scores?"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 7, "t": "Part 3 · MCQs (1–10)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Data dictionary and data literacy. QUESTIONS 1–10 1. A data dictionary describes… 2. Which is NOT normally part of a data dictionary entry? 3. “Rating: 1–5, where 5 = strongly agree” is best described as… 4. A field “Major” with values Business, Design, Media is… 5. The dictionary says Hours_studied is 0–80. A value of 230 should be… 6. Why record a unit such as “minutes” in a data dictionary? 7. The “Source” entry of a field tells you… 8. Student_ID should be treated as… 9. Morrow’s four pillars are reading, working with, analysing and… 10. Raw values organised with context become…"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 8, "t": "Part 3 · MCQs (11–20)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Data literacy and data protection. QUESTIONS 11–20 1. Which is unstructured data? 2. Support rose from 30% to 36%. This is… 3. For house prices with a few very expensive villas, the best “typical” value is the… 4. Two variables rise together. You can conclude that they are… 5. Asking “who is missing from this dataset?” is an example of… 6. The data skills gap is the difference between… 7. The UAE Personal Data Protection Law took effect on… 8. Under the UAE PDPL, the default legal basis for processing personal data is… 9. Which right does the UAE PDPL give individuals? 10. Which UAE zones have their own data protection laws?"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 9, "t": "Part 3 · MCQs (21–30)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Data protection and surveys. QUESTIONS 21–30 1. Replacing names with codes while a key can re-link them is… 2. Re-identification means… 3. Valid consent should be… 4. The whole group you want to draw conclusions about is the… 5. Every member has an equal chance of selection in a… 6. Grouping students by year and sampling randomly within each year is… 7. People who reply differ from those who do not. This is… 8. A 1–5 scale from “strongly disagree” to “strongly agree” is a… 9. Testing a survey on a few people before launch is a… 10. Surveying only your friends because they are easy to reach is…"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 10, "t": "Part 4 · Find the error and fix it (1–10)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Name the error, then rewrite the question. QUESTIONS 1–10 Q1 “How satisfied are you with the gym’s equipment and opening hours?” Q2 “How often do you skip breakfast before class?” Q3 “Do you exercise regularly?” Q4 “Age: 18–20 / 20–22 / 22–25” Q5 “How do you travel to campus? Car / Bus” Q6 “Most students love the new timetable. Do you?” Q7 “Do you never not attend lectures?” Q8 “How would you rate the LMS’s UX and SSO integration?” Q9 “Rate the library: Excellent / Very good / Good / Poor” Q10 “How many hours did you study on each day of last semester?”"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 11, "t": "Part 4 · Find the error and fix it (11–20)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Name the error, then rewrite the question. QUESTIONS 11–20 Q1 “Do you always check your email?” Q2 “Should the university improve its outdated website?” Q3 “Why do you prefer online classes?” Q4 “Was the orientation useful and well organised?” Q5 “Rate from 1 to 10 (1 = best).” Q6 “Do you agree that the bus service is good?” Q7 “How satisfied are you with the course? Very satisfied / Satisfied” Q8 “What do you think about parking?” Q9 “Given rising costs, should tuition increase?” Q10 “In the past, how often have you used the career centre?”"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 12, "t": "Part 4 · Find the error and fix it (21–30)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Name the error, then rewrite the question. QUESTIONS 21–30 Q1 “Are you happy with your grades and your advisor?” Q2 “How much do you spend on books? ____” Q3 “Do you use the library? Yes / No” Q4 “Which social media app do you use? (choose one)” Q5 “Experts say sleep improves grades. How many hours do you sleep?” Q6 “How likely are you to not recommend this course?” Q7 “Rate the lecture’s clarity, pace and examples (1–5).” Q8 “Year of study: 1 / 2 / 3” Q9 “Do you agree or disagree that group work should not be removed?” Q10 “How often do you use the shuttle? Often / Sometimes / Rarely”"}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 13, "t": "Part 5 · Five principles (1–10)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Choose the principle most at stake. QUESTIONS 1–10 1. A fitness app shares users’ heart-rate data with partners without asking them. 2. An airline uses booking data to set personalised prices but never mentions this anywhere. 3. A clinic emails a spreadsheet of patient results to the wrong mailing list. 4. Before launching a study app, the team writes down why each field is needed and how it could harm users. 5. A navigation app’s shortcuts send heavy traffic through a quiet residential street. 6. A smart TV records viewing habits by default, and users cannot switch it off. 7. A smart speaker’s 60-page policy never mentions that staff listen to some recordings. 8. A researcher publishes “anonymous” data that still contain birth dates and postcodes. 9. A school plans to collect students’ face images with no defined purpose. 10. A scholarship formula, built in good faith, ends up favouring students from wealthy areas."}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 14, "t": "Part 5 · Five principles (11–20)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Choose the principle most at stake. QUESTIONS 11–20 1. A user asks a streaming service to delete her account data and is refused. 2. A city installs number-plate cameras with no signs telling drivers. 3. A gym’s member file is shared as “anyone with the link”. 4. A marketing firm plans to collect teenagers’ data to target them with gambling-style game ads. 5. A course-recommendation app gradually pushes students into narrower subject choices. 6. A sign-up form has the “share my data with partners” box already ticked. 7. A retailer starts using customer data in a new way and does not inform customers. 8. A hotel stores guests’ passport scans on an unencrypted shared drive. 9. A city collects location data to plan bus routes and decides in advance not to use it for anything else. 10. Patrols increase in one neighbourhood after a prediction tool, which then records more incidents there."}, {"p": "practice-midterm.html", "s": 15, "t": "Part 5 · Five principles (21–30)", "w": "Midterm practice · Midterm Practice", "x": "Choose the principle most at stake. QUESTIONS 21–30 1. A hospital lets patients download a copy of their own records. 2. An HR team screens CVs with software, but candidates are never told software is used. 3. A class list with students’ phone numbers is posted publicly. 4. An employer considers monitoring staff keystrokes “just to see what we find”. 5. A face-recognition gate fails more often for some users, delaying them every day. 6. A game keeps collecting location after the player withdraws consent. 7. A research team publishes its data-collection method so others can check it. 8. Results are aggregated to faculty level before publication so no student can be identified. 9. A survey designer first asks: what decision will these answers support? 10. An automatic timetable gives evening classes mostly to working students, who then drop out more."}, {"p": "practice-quiz1.html", "s": 0, "t": "Part A · MCQs", "w": "Practice Quiz 1 · Practice Quiz 1", "x": "Choose one answer. Question 2 accepts two answers. FIVE QUESTIONS 1. Which statement best describes data governance? 2. A hospital links patient feedback forms with appointment records to study waiting times. This represents… 3. Which is good data governance practice? 4. Two departments report different student numbers for the same term. This shows the lack of… 5. Which presentation shows data literacy?"}, {"p": "practice-quiz1.html", "s": 1, "t": "Part B · Classify each question", "w": "Practice Quiz 1 · Practice Quiz 1", "x": "Name the question type and explain why. THREE QUESTIONS a) “On a scale of 0–10, how satisfied are you with the shuttle service?” b) “Describe one problem you had when registering for courses.” c) “Surely you agree our new portal is the best in the country?”"}, {"p": "practice-quiz1.html", "s": 2, "t": "Part B · Improve a survey", "w": "Practice Quiz 1 · Practice Quiz 1", "x": "You are designing a satisfaction survey for visitors to a city’s public libraries. Propose a sampling method and one bias to avoid. TWO PARTS Sampling method One bias and how to reduce it"}, {"p": "practice-quiz1.html", "s": 3, "t": "Part C · Read the table", "w": "Practice Quiz 1 · Practice Quiz 1", "x": "An online bookstore summarised one month of orders. ORDERS BY CATEGORY CATEGORY SHARE OF ORDERS AVERAGE RATING (1–5) Fiction 30% 4.1 Textbooks 28% 3.6 Children’s books 18% 4.7 Stationery 16% 4.3 Other 8% 3.4 a) Which category contributes most to order volume? b) Which category has the highest satisfaction, and why should management notice it? c) Suggest one data-driven decision. d) Which type of analytics is this table?"}], "terms": [{"g": "Variable", "d": "A characteristic that can change: age, major, rating (a column)"}, {"g": "Observation", "d": "One record: one person or event (a row)"}, {"g": "Categorical", "d": "Values are groups or labels: major, city"}, {"g": "Numerical", "d": "Values are numbers you can calculate with: hours, price"}, {"g": "Mean", "d": "Add all values and divide by how many"}, {"g": "Median", "d": "The middle value when sorted"}, {"g": "Mode", "d": "The most common value"}, {"g": "Range", "d": "Largest minus smallest"}, {"g": "Outlier", "d": "A value far from the others"}, {"g": "Percentage points", "d": "The difference between two percentages: 40% → 50% is +10 points (+25%)"}, {"g": "Correlation", "d": "Two variables move together"}, {"g": "Causation", "d": "One variable actually changes the other"}, {"g": "Data", "d": "Recorded observations"}, {"g": "Information", "d": "Data organised with context"}, {"g": "Structured data", "d": "Fits rows and columns"}, {"g": "Digital economy", "d": "Economic activity that depends on digital technology and data"}, {"g": "Data skills gap", "d": "Skills employers need minus skills workers have"}, {"g": "Four pillars", "d": "Reading, working with, analysing, communicating with data"}, {"g": "Tidy data", "d": "One variable per column, one observation per row"}, {"g": "Non-response bias", "d": "Those who reply differ from those who do not"}, {"g": "Loaded question", "d": "Assumes something that may not be true"}, {"g": "Descriptive analytics", "d": "What happened?"}, {"g": "Diagnostic analytics", "d": "Why did it happen?"}, {"g": "Predictive analytics", "d": "What will happen?"}, {"g": "Prescriptive analytics", "d": "What should we do?"}, {"g": "Data dictionary", "d": "Name, type, unit, allowed values, definition, source"}, {"g": "Three Cs", "d": "Curiosity, creativity, critical thinking"}, {"g": "Re-identification", "d": "Finding people in data without names"}, {"g": "Proxy variable", "d": "A stand-in for what you want to measure"}, {"g": "Cherry-picking", "d": "Showing only the data that supports a story"}]};
